  "worked_solution": "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={log}_{2}\u2061x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({log}_{2}\u2061x)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{log}_{2}^{2}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(x)=2x+2{log}_{2}\u2061x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2{log}_{2}\u2061x}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
